This repository vendors a prebuilt binary of GoReSym:

    Project:  GoReSym
    Author:   Mandiant (Google Cloud) FLARE team
    Source:   https://github.com/mandiant/GoReSym
    Commit:   722897e0a80da0a06c00c5e40591a253a551f04e
    License:  MIT (see goresym_service/vendor/GoReSym_LICENSE, copied verbatim)

goresym_service/vendor/GoReSym is a prebuilt Linux binary built from that project's
source at the commit above, WITH ONE MODIFICATION to main.go: see
goresym_service/vendor/moduledata_fallback.patch (a plain unified diff against
upstream's main.go) for the exact change, summarized here per MIT's attribution
requirements:

Upstream's extraction pipeline (main.go, main_impl) requires two independent things
to both succeed before it will report anything: (1) a pclntab candidate whose header
validates (correct magic/quantum/pointer-size), AND (2) a corroborating `moduledata`
structure found by searching the whole file for the literal pointer-to-pclntab byte
value. If (2) fails for every candidate, upstream discards even a perfectly valid (1)
and reports "no valid pclntab found" -- function and file metadata is fully
recoverable from the pclntab alone, without moduledata, but upstream never gets that
far.

This was not a hypothetical: a real, otherwise-unremarkable Go 1.27.1 Windows PE
sample was confirmed (by manually re-implementing the same header validation upstream
uses, then confirming the expected moduledata pointer value genuinely does not appear
anywhere in the file) to have exactly this problem. The patch tracks the first
candidate whose header validated even when no moduledata could be found, and falls
back to it -- recovering full user/std function names and embedded file paths (217
and 253 respectively on that sample) instead of a flat failure. Type/interface
recovery is correctly skipped in this fallback case, since that specifically requires
moduledata's typelinks/itablinks. The normal, moduledata-found path (and everything
downstream of it, e.g. type recovery) is untouched -- verified by running the patched
binary against GoReSym's own binary, a real Go binary that already worked before this
change, and confirming Types/UserFunctions counts are identical to upstream.

It is invoked as a subprocess and its output is parsed by this service's own original
code (goresym_service/runner.py, goresym_service/goresym_service.py), which is
licensed under this repository's own MIT LICENSE.

GoReSym itself depends on a few other MIT-licensed projects (orderedmap by
elliotchance, binaryregexp by rsc, and the Go standard library/runtime source); see
the upstream project's README and its own `-about` output for those attributions.
